-- title:
--   Ramified special λ-adic realisation at q ∥ M
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number with $M \neq 0$ and let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform in the project's sense: $g$ is a normalised eigenform (its $q$-expansion coefficients satisfy $a_1(g)=1$, $a_{mn}=a_ma_n$ for coprime $m,n$, and the recursions $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_pa_{p^{r+1}}-p\,a_{p^r}$ for primes $p \nmid M$ and $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_pa_{p^{r+1}}$ for $p \mid M$) and for no proper divisor $M'$ of $M$ does there exist a normalised eigenform of weight two on $\Gamma_0(M')$ with the same $\ell$-th coefficients for all primes $\ell \nmid M$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, $S$ a finite set of naturals, and $O'$ a discrete valuation domain of characteristic zero, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, with finite residue field and with $\lambda$ in its maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism to $\mathbb{C}$ from the Hecke algebra of weight two and level $M$ away from $S$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of the cusp forms generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid M$, $\ell \notin S$, and $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, $q \notin S$) satisfying $\chi_g(T_\ell)=a_\ell(g)$ for all such $\ell$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the image of $\chi_g$ to $O'$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q \neq \lambda$, $q \mid M$ and $q^2 \nmid M$, and let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$, meaning that $q$ is a nonunit of $P$. Then there is a type $O''$, again a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field, carrying an $O'$-algebra structure which makes it a finite $O'$-module with injective local structure map, and there is an adically continuous two-dimensional representation $\rho$ over $O''$ (a finite free $O''$-module $V$ with $\operatorname{rank} V = 2$ and a monoid homomorphism $\rho$ from the $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $\operatorname{End}_{O''}(V)$ trivial modulo each power of the maximal ideal on the automorphisms fixing a suitable number field) such that the following hold. First, for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $\ell \notin S$ and $\ell \neq \lambda$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $\ell$ and every $\sigma$ in the decomposition group of $A$ acting on the residue field of $A$ as $x \mapsto x^\ell$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ equals $X^2 - \iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))X + \ell$, the coefficients being taken in $O''$ via $O' \to O''$. Second, some $\tau$ in the inertia subgroup at $P$ (the image in the automorphism group of the inertia subgroup of the decomposition group) moves some vector of $V$. Third, there are an $O''$-submodule $L$ of $V$ and an integer $a \in \{1,-1\}$ with $a_q(g)=a$ in $\mathbb{C}$, such that $L$ is the $O''$-span of the first member of some $O''$-basis of $V$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,2$; $L$ is stable under the decomposition group at $P$; every $\tau$ in the inertia subgroup at $P$ fixes $L$ pointwise and satisfies $\rho(\tau)v - v \in L$ for all $v \in V$; and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $q$ for $P$ acts on $L$ by the scalar $aq$ and satisfies $\rho(\sigma)v - a\,v \in L$ for all $v \in V$.
--
--   This is the local–global compatibility statement at a prime exactly dividing the level: the $\lambda$-adic representation attached to a weight-two newform $g$ on $\Gamma_0(M)$, realised over a finite extension of the chosen coefficient ring, is special at $q \parallel M$, with unramified quotient on which Frobenius acts by $a_q(g) = \pm 1$ and, in addition, non-trivial monodromy at $q$. It strengthens the corresponding existence statement without the ramification clause by that single conjunct, and is used in the analysis of the local behaviour at primes of bad reduction in the level-lowering part of the argument.
